<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Western Yan</h1><p class="st-meta">384 CE – 394 CE · capital Changzi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A wandering Murong Xianbei state born in the Former Qin collapse, which sacked Chang'an and then settled in Shanxi until Later Yan destroyed it in 394.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a>'s defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Fei River</a> in 383, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Hong, a prince of the deposed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a> house, rebelled in 384 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, where many <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> had been resettled. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chong">Murong Chong</a> took over, captured and plundered Chang'an in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Western Yan was less a settled state than a migrating host of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei</a> soldiers and families, longing to return east. Its leaders were murdered in quick succession by their own followers. In 386 the host moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-yong">Murong Yong</a> established himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changzi">Changzi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-yong">Murong Yong</a> refused to submit to his relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui of Later Yan</a>, who regarded himself as the rightful heir of the Yan line. In 394 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Chui invaded, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changzi">Changzi</a> and executed Murong Yong.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/western-yan.jpg" alt="Western Yan" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_391_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>384 CE – 394 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Changzi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Murong Chong, Murong Yong</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xianbei</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yan (Murong Hong)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=389&amp;focus=western-yan" aria-label="Western Yan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7449 935 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Western Yan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7769 1191L7769 1191M7769 1191L8033 1602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M8033 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7769 1191h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=389&amp;focus=western-yan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
